WEBVTT

00:00:00.810 --> 00:00:02.100
KLORIDA MIRAJ: Hey, everyone.

00:00:02.100 --> 00:00:03.360
Welcome to Build.

00:00:03.360 --> 00:00:06.540
I'm Klorida from the Windows
Developer Platform team,

00:00:06.540 --> 00:00:11.070
and I'm here with Sakib from
Windows Enterprise and Security.

00:00:11.070 --> 00:00:12.000
NAZMUS SAKIB: Hi.

00:00:12.000 --> 00:00:13.530
KLORIDA MIRAJ: Today
we want to talk

00:00:13.530 --> 00:00:16.530
about how Windows can
help developers build

00:00:16.530 --> 00:00:18.780
and run agents safely at scale.

00:00:18.780 --> 00:00:22.500
We are at a moment where
agents are starting to feel

00:00:22.500 --> 00:00:24.750
like a new unit of software.

00:00:24.750 --> 00:00:27.330
They are not just
answering questions.

00:00:27.330 --> 00:00:31.020
They can reason, use
tools, remember context.

00:00:31.020 --> 00:00:34.140
And, increasingly, they're
taking action in ways

00:00:34.140 --> 00:00:37.140
that feel more
collaborative and alive.

00:00:37.140 --> 00:00:41.460
So the experience is starting
to shift from asking software

00:00:41.460 --> 00:00:45.810
to do something for you to
actually building with it

00:00:45.810 --> 00:00:48.690
and evolving it over time.

00:00:48.690 --> 00:00:52.650
That is why I love the
Terrarium metaphor.

00:00:52.650 --> 00:00:55.590
It is not only about automation.

00:00:55.590 --> 00:00:57.600
It is about creativity,

00:00:57.600 --> 00:01:01.680
customization, and
shared building.

00:01:01.680 --> 00:01:04.380
You start with something
someone else built,

00:01:04.380 --> 00:01:08.280
you make it your own, and then
you keep building together.

00:01:08.280 --> 00:01:11.400
And that matters because it
changes what people expect

00:01:11.400 --> 00:01:13.230
from software.

00:01:13.230 --> 00:01:16.740
Instead of just consuming
a finished product,

00:01:16.740 --> 00:01:20.430
people are increasingly
shaping the experience.

00:01:20.430 --> 00:01:23.190
They're adapting it
to their own world

00:01:23.190 --> 00:01:26.160
and sharing those changes
back with each other.

00:01:26.160 --> 00:01:29.730
That is a much more
participatory model and one

00:01:29.730 --> 00:01:33.450
that makes agents feel less
like a feature and more

00:01:33.450 --> 00:01:36.330
like a medium for collaboration.

00:01:36.330 --> 00:01:39.930
So there's the real
story behind this slide.

00:01:39.930 --> 00:01:43.170
Agent Terrarium here
is a project a friend

00:01:43.170 --> 00:01:46.380
of mine, Alex Clarke, created.

00:01:46.380 --> 00:01:49.200
What he did is he built
the initial world.

00:01:49.200 --> 00:01:52.050
He shared the GitHub
repo with me,

00:01:52.050 --> 00:01:55.740
and what I did is I extended
it with my own theme.

00:01:55.740 --> 00:02:00.270
You see here on the bottom right
corner there is the Albanian

00:02:00.270 --> 00:02:01.830
mountains theme.

00:02:01.830 --> 00:02:05.760
And I also added Zana --
this is an Albanian fairy --

00:02:05.760 --> 00:02:07.230
as an agent.

00:02:07.230 --> 00:02:11.120
Then I shared my version
back, and now we both build

00:02:11.120 --> 00:02:13.100
on top of each other's work.

00:02:13.100 --> 00:02:18.720
It is fun, and it's also made
our collaboration better.

00:02:18.720 --> 00:02:22.830
And that is where the
platform matters.

00:02:22.830 --> 00:02:27.840
As agents become something
that people can shape, extend,

00:02:27.840 --> 00:02:31.020
and collaborate with, the
operating system has to do more

00:02:31.020 --> 00:02:33.030
than just host them, right?

00:02:33.030 --> 00:02:35.220
It has to remove the friction;

00:02:35.220 --> 00:02:40.970
and it has to also provide a
safe, governable environment

00:02:40.970 --> 00:02:45.650
where people together can
create and they can run

00:02:45.650 --> 00:02:48.750
and they can build
things with confidence.

00:02:48.750 --> 00:02:51.690
That is why Windows
matters here, right,

00:02:51.690 --> 00:02:55.410
and why we believe it is
the best platform to run

00:02:55.410 --> 00:02:57.510
and build agents at scale.

00:02:57.510 --> 00:03:01.260
Now, that matters because
customers are no longer just

00:03:01.260 --> 00:03:04.590
asking whether agents can
do something impressive

00:03:04.590 --> 00:03:06.510
or something cool, right?

00:03:06.510 --> 00:03:09.390
They're asking whether
those agents can be trusted

00:03:09.390 --> 00:03:12.720
in these real environments,
on their actual machines

00:03:12.720 --> 00:03:16.200
that they're running, with
real business data, right?

00:03:16.200 --> 00:03:22.140
So Windows has to answer these
two questions at the same time.

00:03:22.140 --> 00:03:25.740
Can the agent be powerful;
and, at the same time,

00:03:25.740 --> 00:03:29.760
can the platform still
keep it governable?

00:03:29.760 --> 00:03:34.860
Let's start first with what
makes agents different.

00:03:34.860 --> 00:03:37.800
So agents are non-deterministic.

00:03:37.800 --> 00:03:38.850
We know that.

00:03:38.850 --> 00:03:43.050
They operate on data that can
itself contain instructions

00:03:43.050 --> 00:03:45.690
or some adversarial content.

00:03:45.690 --> 00:03:50.280
And they can act fast,
at machine speed fast.

00:03:50.280 --> 00:03:54.900
That means that even a
mistake or a bad inference

00:03:54.900 --> 00:03:58.800
or an attack can
scale so much faster

00:03:58.800 --> 00:04:03.150
than in more traditional
user-driven type of flows.

00:04:03.150 --> 00:04:07.980
The key shift is that this is no
longer just a prompt-and-answer

00:04:07.980 --> 00:04:09.330
problem, right?

00:04:09.330 --> 00:04:12.330
It is an execution problem.

00:04:12.330 --> 00:04:14.220
Agents can read files.

00:04:14.220 --> 00:04:15.750
They can invoke tools.

00:04:15.750 --> 00:04:17.610
They can call services.

00:04:17.610 --> 00:04:21.240
They can modify entire
environments, right?

00:04:21.240 --> 00:04:24.390
And that means that the risk
is not just what the model

00:04:24.390 --> 00:04:25.740
is saying.

00:04:25.740 --> 00:04:28.560
The risk is where
execution happens,

00:04:28.560 --> 00:04:30.690
what the agent can reach,

00:04:30.690 --> 00:04:34.980
and how much authority
it inherits by default.

00:04:34.980 --> 00:04:38.790
Once you understand the risk
model that I just explained,

00:04:38.790 --> 00:04:42.840
right, the first three
needs are super clear:

00:04:42.840 --> 00:04:48.630
containment to limit the blast
radius, detection and response

00:04:48.630 --> 00:04:52.920
to catch problems early, and
then the agent identities

00:04:52.920 --> 00:04:55.170
that are distinct from
humans so permissions

00:04:55.170 --> 00:04:59.160
and attribution
remain meaningful.

00:04:59.160 --> 00:05:02.430
These are not abstract
architecture ideas, right?

00:05:02.430 --> 00:05:05.550
They're what makes the
difference between an agent

00:05:05.550 --> 00:05:09.090
that feels impressive
in a demo and one

00:05:09.090 --> 00:05:13.410
that can actually be trusted
in a real environment.

00:05:13.410 --> 00:05:17.040
Governance is the one that
completes that model.

00:05:17.040 --> 00:05:22.050
Agents need to be manageable,
discoverable, observable,

00:05:22.050 --> 00:05:25.470
and configurable in
enterprise environments.

00:05:25.470 --> 00:05:28.560
These are not separate check
boxes that you'll have

00:05:28.560 --> 00:05:30.180
to go and check, right?

00:05:30.180 --> 00:05:34.770
Together, containment,
detection, identity,

00:05:34.770 --> 00:05:37.350
and governance form
the foundation

00:05:37.350 --> 00:05:41.010
for trustworthy agentic systems.

00:05:41.010 --> 00:05:41.580
All right.

00:05:41.580 --> 00:05:44.310
It's time for me to
hand it to Sakib.

00:05:44.310 --> 00:05:46.980
He's going to go through
the containment details.

00:05:46.980 --> 00:05:48.420
NAZMUS SAKIB: Thanks, Klorida.

00:05:48.420 --> 00:05:51.630
Now that we framed the
problem, let's get concrete

00:05:51.630 --> 00:05:54.090
about containment and the
Windows building blocks

00:05:54.090 --> 00:05:56.790
that make safer agents possible.

00:05:56.790 --> 00:06:00.930
Containment is one of the most
important ideas in this talk.

00:06:00.930 --> 00:06:03.960
If agent behavior is
non-deterministic,

00:06:03.960 --> 00:06:07.140
then containment becomes
the deterministic guardrail

00:06:07.140 --> 00:06:08.520
around it.

00:06:08.520 --> 00:06:12.000
The reason containment matters
is simple: Agents should be able

00:06:12.000 --> 00:06:16.110
to do useful work without
inheriting the full blast radius

00:06:16.110 --> 00:06:18.060
of the user's session.

00:06:18.060 --> 00:06:20.670
In both ordinary and
adversarial cases,

00:06:20.670 --> 00:06:23.340
containment reduces
the blast radius

00:06:23.340 --> 00:06:26.940
and constrains what a
risky operation can touch.

00:06:26.940 --> 00:06:28.020
That is the shift

00:06:28.020 --> 00:06:31.500
from traditional software
to agentic software.

00:06:31.500 --> 00:06:33.840
In traditional apps, a
mistake is often bounded

00:06:33.840 --> 00:06:35.400
by the feature itself.

00:06:35.400 --> 00:06:37.860
With agents, the execution
surface is larger

00:06:37.860 --> 00:06:40.200
because the system
can read, act,

00:06:40.200 --> 00:06:42.630
and chain together
multiple steps.

00:06:42.630 --> 00:06:46.530
Containment is how you keep that
larger surface from turning

00:06:46.530 --> 00:06:48.900
into a larger blast radius.

00:06:48.900 --> 00:06:51.840
Given the importance of
containment, we are excited

00:06:51.840 --> 00:06:56.250
to introduce the Microsoft
Execution Containers SDK.

00:06:56.250 --> 00:07:00.060
This single SDK will allow
applying containment policies

00:07:00.060 --> 00:07:03.150
on agents enforced by the OS.

00:07:03.150 --> 00:07:06.420
The MXC SDK will provide
an abstraction layer

00:07:06.420 --> 00:07:09.120
across various
isolation primitives

00:07:09.120 --> 00:07:13.110
that simplifies containing many
different types of workloads.

00:07:13.110 --> 00:07:15.390
Microsoft execution
containers are built

00:07:15.390 --> 00:07:18.690
around a composable
sandbox model.

00:07:18.690 --> 00:07:21.090
The same policy and SDK can map

00:07:21.090 --> 00:07:23.370
to different isolation
boundaries depending

00:07:23.370 --> 00:07:26.400
on the workload, so
developers are not locked

00:07:26.400 --> 00:07:28.350
into one rigid container.

00:07:28.350 --> 00:07:30.750
Windows can apply the
right isolation primitive

00:07:30.750 --> 00:07:35.130
for the task while preserving
one coherent abstraction.

00:07:35.130 --> 00:07:38.730
Different agents and tools need
different isolation levels.

00:07:38.730 --> 00:07:42.000
Our goal is simple: Let
developers declare intent

00:07:42.000 --> 00:07:45.570
with policies, and let
Windows enforce the

00:07:45.570 --> 00:07:47.550
appropriate boundary.

00:07:47.550 --> 00:07:49.770
What makes this
model powerful is

00:07:49.770 --> 00:07:52.020
that it meets developers
where they are.

00:07:52.020 --> 00:07:54.870
A Coding Agent, an
interactive agent,

00:07:54.870 --> 00:07:57.600
and a cloud-managed agent do
not all need the same boundary,

00:07:57.600 --> 00:08:01.170
but they do need one
coherent trust story.

00:08:01.170 --> 00:08:03.990
That is what the composable
sandbox gives you:

00:08:03.990 --> 00:08:05.790
flexibility in implementation

00:08:05.790 --> 00:08:08.760
without fragmentation
in governance.

00:08:08.760 --> 00:08:09.960
For developers,

00:08:09.960 --> 00:08:13.290
the implementation story is
practical and incremental.

00:08:13.290 --> 00:08:15.450
Think of a four-stage plan.

00:08:15.450 --> 00:08:19.140
First, plan by identifying the
sensitive operations your agent

00:08:19.140 --> 00:08:22.230
needs to perform, especially
ones that may be exposed

00:08:22.230 --> 00:08:26.040
to prompt injection risk
or handle customer data.

00:08:26.040 --> 00:08:28.200
Then focus on design.

00:08:28.200 --> 00:08:32.070
Identify the model-driven parts
so that they can be contained.

00:08:32.070 --> 00:08:35.550
With the design in hand, you
can then move to implement.

00:08:35.550 --> 00:08:39.900
Contain the highest-risk
operations with our MXC SDK.

00:08:39.900 --> 00:08:43.320
Finally, refine your solution
by testing what is allowed

00:08:43.320 --> 00:08:46.980
and denied; and ensure that
it meets your experience

00:08:46.980 --> 00:08:48.780
and security goals.

00:08:48.780 --> 00:08:51.330
Containment is not a bolt-on.

00:08:51.330 --> 00:08:54.920
It is a design tool for
building safer agentic apps

00:08:54.920 --> 00:08:56.360
from the beginning.

00:08:56.360 --> 00:08:58.940
Containment should
not add friction.

00:08:58.940 --> 00:09:02.690
A single SDK and policy
model make it easier

00:09:02.690 --> 00:09:05.340
to adopt the right
isolation level.

00:09:05.340 --> 00:09:06.780
KLORIDA MIRAJ: Thanks, Sakib.

00:09:06.780 --> 00:09:10.320
The composable sandbox is the
big idea that you're going

00:09:10.320 --> 00:09:13.680
to see in the next several
slides here with me.

00:09:13.680 --> 00:09:17.490
Windows is not giving
developers one rigid container

00:09:17.490 --> 00:09:21.630
and asking them to force
every workload into it.

00:09:21.630 --> 00:09:24.810
It's offering a spectrum
of containment options

00:09:24.810 --> 00:09:28.490
so the boundary can match
the risk of the task.

00:09:28.490 --> 00:09:31.280
It's the same
overall trust model

00:09:31.280 --> 00:09:34.250
but different levels
of isolation.

00:09:34.250 --> 00:09:39.870
This is where the story shifts
from principle to architecture,

00:09:39.870 --> 00:09:44.010
and we are no longer just
saying contain these agents.

00:09:44.010 --> 00:09:48.030
We're showing a practical,
scalable execution model

00:09:48.030 --> 00:09:50.760
that can go from a tool
call on the device

00:09:50.760 --> 00:09:55.530
to a broader agent workflow
across client and cloud.

00:09:55.530 --> 00:09:57.270
So that breadth is one

00:09:57.270 --> 00:10:02.220
of the reasons Windows has a
differentiated story here.

00:10:02.220 --> 00:10:05.490
And that is important
because Windows can start

00:10:05.490 --> 00:10:08.160
with a lightweight
local isolation; and,

00:10:08.160 --> 00:10:12.180
when that is not enough and
then when moved all the way

00:10:12.180 --> 00:10:14.790
to the stronger boundaries
when the workload

00:10:14.790 --> 00:10:18.030
or the enterprise
requirement needs it.

00:10:18.030 --> 00:10:22.020
So we're rolling this initiative
out on a monthly cadence

00:10:22.020 --> 00:10:23.850
with the developer community.

00:10:23.850 --> 00:10:26.250
Some of these capabilities
are already showing

00:10:26.250 --> 00:10:29.250
up in Windows Insider
builds, and there are more

00:10:29.250 --> 00:10:31.650
that are going to come
later this month.

00:10:31.650 --> 00:10:34.440
And we'll keep building
and learning together

00:10:34.440 --> 00:10:36.830
as the platform evolves.

00:10:36.830 --> 00:10:40.220
We're excited, and
I know you are too.

00:10:40.220 --> 00:10:42.260
So let's dig a little bit deeper

00:10:42.260 --> 00:10:44.900
into the containment
spectrum now.

00:10:44.900 --> 00:10:47.940
I'm going to start
with a micro-VM.

00:10:47.940 --> 00:10:52.560
So at the stronger end of the
spectrum is the micro-VM.

00:10:52.560 --> 00:10:56.750
This is the right fit when
the workload is higher risk

00:10:56.750 --> 00:11:02.030
and it needs a clearly isolated
space while still preserving the

00:11:02.030 --> 00:11:06.180
same overall agent experience.

00:11:06.180 --> 00:11:10.590
And the message is not to
use this everywhere, right?

00:11:10.590 --> 00:11:16.200
It's to step up isolation when
the workload calls for it.

00:11:16.200 --> 00:11:19.560
Micro-VMs are also
about density.

00:11:19.560 --> 00:11:23.010
They provide a stronger
separation without having

00:11:23.010 --> 00:11:27.750
to pay the full cost of a
larger VM every single time

00:11:27.750 --> 00:11:30.720
that you're running your agent.

00:11:30.720 --> 00:11:33.510
I just explained the
Hyperlight micro-VM,

00:11:33.510 --> 00:11:35.610
the execution
primitive at the top

00:11:35.610 --> 00:11:37.560
of the agent containment stack.

00:11:37.560 --> 00:11:40.170
Let me show you what that
looks like in practice.

00:11:40.170 --> 00:11:43.050
Imagine Maya, a freelance
interior designer.

00:11:43.050 --> 00:11:45.090
She's been using
GitHub Copilot CLI

00:11:45.090 --> 00:11:46.770
to manage her client projects,

00:11:46.770 --> 00:11:49.200
and her previous task
has just wrapped up.

00:11:49.200 --> 00:11:52.860
She has three client PDFs with
comments and review feedback

00:11:52.860 --> 00:11:54.750
that she needs to consolidate.

00:11:54.750 --> 00:11:57.300
Let's watch her ask
Copilot for help.

00:11:57.300 --> 00:11:59.730
She types a natural
language request:

00:11:59.730 --> 00:12:02.700
Pull the review comments
from all three client PDFs,

00:12:02.700 --> 00:12:06.150
group them by theme, and produce
a clean prioritized summary.

00:12:06.150 --> 00:12:08.700
No scripts to write,
no tools to configure.

00:12:08.700 --> 00:12:11.100
It's just a plain-English ask.

00:12:11.100 --> 00:12:13.500
Copilot understands
the task immediately,

00:12:13.500 --> 00:12:15.510
and it lays out its plan.

00:12:15.510 --> 00:12:16.470
Notice that it asks

00:12:16.470 --> 00:12:19.080
for permission before
accessing Maya's files.

00:12:19.080 --> 00:12:21.000
The user stays in control.

00:12:21.000 --> 00:12:24.060
Maya grants access, and
now Copilot gets to work.

00:12:24.060 --> 00:12:26.370
Behind the scenes, Copilot
is figuring out how

00:12:26.370 --> 00:12:28.920
to extract annotations
from those PDFs.

00:12:28.920 --> 00:12:31.200
It needs to run code
to parse them, right?

00:12:31.200 --> 00:12:35.610
But here's the key question:
Where does that code execute?

00:12:35.610 --> 00:12:36.930
There it is.

00:12:36.930 --> 00:12:41.010
Extract PDF annotations
in Hyperlight micro-VM.

00:12:41.010 --> 00:12:44.700
Copilot chose to run this code
inside a Hyperlight micro-VM,

00:12:44.700 --> 00:12:47.580
and I hope you didn't
miss the wxc exe

00:12:47.580 --> 00:12:49.260
that appeared in Task Manager.

00:12:49.260 --> 00:12:51.780
That's the micro-VM
host process.

00:12:51.780 --> 00:12:54.330
And, once the task is
done, Copilot cleans

00:12:54.330 --> 00:12:55.620
up the temporary config; and,

00:12:55.620 --> 00:12:57.420
just like that, the
micro-VM is gone.

00:12:57.420 --> 00:12:59.610
No traces left behind.

00:12:59.610 --> 00:13:02.760
But notice in the File
Explorer there's a new file

00:13:02.760 --> 00:13:05.910
that has appeared:
Client Feedback Summary.

00:13:05.910 --> 00:13:08.430
Copilot has delivered a
well-organized summary,

00:13:08.430 --> 00:13:11.310
comments from all three
client PDFs, grouped by theme

00:13:11.310 --> 00:13:13.080
and ranked by priority,

00:13:13.080 --> 00:13:16.170
all of this processed inside
a Hyperlight micro-VM,

00:13:16.170 --> 00:13:19.230
hardware-level isolation
with sub-second startup.

00:13:19.230 --> 00:13:20.970
That's the power of MXC,

00:13:20.970 --> 00:13:24.810
safe code execution that's
fast enough to be invisible.

00:13:24.810 --> 00:13:27.240
Process isolation,
on the other side,

00:13:27.240 --> 00:13:31.020
sits at this lighter-weight
end of the spectrum.

00:13:31.020 --> 00:13:33.600
It is a strong fit
for coding agents

00:13:33.600 --> 00:13:36.810
and focused tool
execution scenarios

00:13:36.810 --> 00:13:39.660
where speed matters for you.

00:13:39.660 --> 00:13:43.980
So developers keep the familiar
coding flow while Windows

00:13:43.980 --> 00:13:46.620
applies platform-level
boundaries

00:13:46.620 --> 00:13:49.890
to the riskiest operations.

00:13:49.890 --> 00:13:53.580
This is especially important
for developer scenarios

00:13:53.580 --> 00:13:57.150
because the inner loop
needs to stay fast.

00:13:57.150 --> 00:14:00.540
If the smallest,
riskiest operations,

00:14:00.540 --> 00:14:04.080
like running model-generated
code, can be isolated

00:14:04.080 --> 00:14:08.490
at process level, then
developers get a safety benefit

00:14:08.490 --> 00:14:11.700
without losing responsiveness.

00:14:11.700 --> 00:14:14.760
And that is how containment
becomes something developers

00:14:14.760 --> 00:14:18.330
actually adopt rather than
something that they need

00:14:18.330 --> 00:14:22.050
to avoid because it's
slowing them down.

00:14:22.050 --> 00:14:24.330
Now let me pick up where
I left off earlier

00:14:24.330 --> 00:14:26.820
and show you
process-level isolation.

00:14:26.820 --> 00:14:30.570
I have GitHub Copilot CLI with
an experimental feature enabled,

00:14:30.570 --> 00:14:33.300
so code is run in
a process sandbox.

00:14:33.300 --> 00:14:35.130
Going back to Maya,
it's late evening.

00:14:35.130 --> 00:14:37.080
And everyone has gone to bed,

00:14:37.080 --> 00:14:39.270
but she has a
personal task to do.

00:14:39.270 --> 00:14:41.670
Her daughter Laura's
birthday is coming up.

00:14:41.670 --> 00:14:44.490
Maya types "sandbox enabled"
to turn on sandboxing.

00:14:44.490 --> 00:14:47.880
Now every tool execution
Copilot runs will be contained

00:14:47.880 --> 00:14:49.170
in a sandbox.

00:14:49.170 --> 00:14:52.650
She then asks Copilot to make a
birthday card using cat photos

00:14:52.650 --> 00:14:54.510
from a folder on her laptop.

00:14:54.510 --> 00:14:57.990
Again, just a natural language
request: Pick the best images.

00:14:57.990 --> 00:14:58.950
Resize them.

00:14:58.950 --> 00:14:59.850
Combine them.

00:14:59.850 --> 00:15:01.620
Add a birthday message.

00:15:01.620 --> 00:15:05.670
Copilot understands and outlines
its approach and requests access

00:15:05.670 --> 00:15:07.650
to the Laura pictures folder.

00:15:07.650 --> 00:15:09.030
Same consent flow.

00:15:09.030 --> 00:15:12.360
The user is always in
the loop and in control.

00:15:12.360 --> 00:15:14.130
Maya grants access.

00:15:14.130 --> 00:15:16.980
And now here's the
sandbox in action.

00:15:16.980 --> 00:15:19.770
You see that blue dot
next to sandbox shell?

00:15:19.770 --> 00:15:23.310
That means this PowerShell code
is executing inside a sandbox.

00:15:23.310 --> 00:15:26.520
The script is loading and
manipulating Maya's cat photos,

00:15:26.520 --> 00:15:28.920
but it's doing so within
a kernel-enforced

00:15:28.920 --> 00:15:30.510
sandbox boundary.

00:15:30.510 --> 00:15:33.900
It can only access what has
been explicitly granted.

00:15:33.900 --> 00:15:36.990
And done. PowerShell
code generated

00:15:36.990 --> 00:15:39.660
and executed all
within the sandbox.

00:15:39.660 --> 00:15:41.820
Notice in the File
Explorer Happy Birthday,

00:15:41.820 --> 00:15:45.540
Laura has appeared right
alongside the source images.

00:15:45.540 --> 00:15:47.160
Let's see what it looks like.

00:15:47.160 --> 00:15:49.050
It's a custom birthday card

00:15:49.050 --> 00:15:52.440
with Maya's favorite cat
photos arranged and captioned,

00:15:52.440 --> 00:15:55.080
all executed in a
process sandbox.

00:15:55.080 --> 00:15:57.420
The code ran, the
file was created,

00:15:57.420 --> 00:16:00.960
and at no point did the
execution escape its boundary.

00:16:00.960 --> 00:16:05.940
This is MXC, Microsoft Execution
Containers, composable sandboxes

00:16:05.940 --> 00:16:08.100
for running dynamically
generated code

00:16:08.100 --> 00:16:10.320
with controlled isolation.

00:16:10.320 --> 00:16:13.800
We're moving on to
Windows and Linux VM.

00:16:13.800 --> 00:16:16.380
A full VM gives
broader separation

00:16:16.380 --> 00:16:18.150
and stronger assurance.

00:16:18.150 --> 00:16:20.040
This is the right
fit for workloads

00:16:20.040 --> 00:16:23.580
that need a different
kernel, complete isolation,

00:16:23.580 --> 00:16:27.240
and stronger guarantees,
especially for high-assurance

00:16:27.240 --> 00:16:30.810
or more controlled
enterprise scenarios.

00:16:30.810 --> 00:16:33.990
This is exactly where
the message now shifts

00:16:33.990 --> 00:16:37.200
from efficiency to assurance.

00:16:37.200 --> 00:16:41.160
A full VM is the answer when
the workload needs much more

00:16:41.160 --> 00:16:43.830
distance from the
host environment,

00:16:43.830 --> 00:16:46.860
whether because the code
is highly untrusted,

00:16:46.860 --> 00:16:49.680
the environment is
maybe tightly managed,

00:16:49.680 --> 00:16:54.600
or the trust requirement is
simply much, much higher.

00:16:54.600 --> 00:16:57.300
This slide shows how
Windows is prepared

00:16:57.300 --> 00:17:00.630
for that higher-assurance end
of the spectrum, too, right.

00:17:00.630 --> 00:17:04.080
For enterprise customers,
this is where policy

00:17:04.080 --> 00:17:09.180
and assurance start to matter
even more than convenience.

00:17:09.180 --> 00:17:13.860
A full VM gives organizations a
boundary they already understand

00:17:13.860 --> 00:17:15.420
and one they can reason

00:17:15.420 --> 00:17:19.380
about when the workload
is especially sensitive

00:17:19.380 --> 00:17:22.980
or if the cost of
compromise is high.

00:17:22.980 --> 00:17:27.480
Cloud VMs extend containment
beyond the local device.

00:17:27.480 --> 00:17:31.770
Now, in the enterprise, this
can be a Windows 365-powered,

00:17:31.770 --> 00:17:34.950
giving the agent a cloud
PC that is separate

00:17:34.950 --> 00:17:38.910
from the user's device,
separate from its identity,

00:17:38.910 --> 00:17:42.510
separate from the user's
kernel device and session.

00:17:42.510 --> 00:17:45.990
The agent runs remotely
in a managed environment,

00:17:45.990 --> 00:17:49.680
not alongside the user
on the same machine.

00:17:49.680 --> 00:17:53.280
And, because it is
built on Windows 365,

00:17:53.280 --> 00:17:56.700
the environment comes with
all the enterprise management

00:17:56.700 --> 00:17:58.620
that is built already in.

00:17:58.620 --> 00:18:02.700
Like security policies that
can be provisioned up front,

00:18:02.700 --> 00:18:06.810
identity compliance can
be managed centrally

00:18:06.810 --> 00:18:10.100
through Microsoft
Entra ID and Intune.

00:18:10.100 --> 00:18:12.980
As you can see, this is
the highest-assurance end

00:18:12.980 --> 00:18:16.070
of the spectrum and
offers the strongest

00:18:16.070 --> 00:18:17.690
blast-radius reduction.

00:18:17.690 --> 00:18:21.080
If the agent is compromised,
the impact is contained

00:18:21.080 --> 00:18:25.490
to a disposable cloud instance
with no direct path back

00:18:25.490 --> 00:18:27.900
to the user's local device.

00:18:27.900 --> 00:18:31.680
So far, we focused on
containment and its spectrum;

00:18:31.680 --> 00:18:34.830
and we showed you that Windows
can support both developer

00:18:34.830 --> 00:18:37.440
productivity and the
enterprise assurance

00:18:37.440 --> 00:18:40.830
by letting the boundary scale
with the workload instead

00:18:40.830 --> 00:18:43.880
of forcing every scenario
into the same model.

00:18:43.880 --> 00:18:46.550
But containment is only
part of this picture.

00:18:46.550 --> 00:18:49.070
Let me hand it back to Sakib
to cover the other side

00:18:49.070 --> 00:18:52.470
of the equation, how
we detect, respond,

00:18:52.470 --> 00:18:57.180
and make the broader security
ecosystem part of the story.

00:18:57.180 --> 00:18:58.440
NAZMUS SAKIB: Thanks, Klorida.

00:18:58.440 --> 00:19:02.940
Agents increasingly depend
on tools, skills, services,

00:19:02.940 --> 00:19:06.270
and sometimes even other
agents to complete a task.

00:19:06.270 --> 00:19:09.270
So developers and administrators
need a clear view

00:19:09.270 --> 00:19:12.510
of what capabilities are
present, what is enabled,

00:19:12.510 --> 00:19:15.350
what is allowed, and
what is being used.

00:19:15.350 --> 00:19:18.470
Local capabilities discovery
provides that source of truth

00:19:18.470 --> 00:19:21.470
and makes those actions
admin-manageable.

00:19:21.470 --> 00:19:24.710
Capabilities discovery matters
because, once agents compose

00:19:24.710 --> 00:19:26.990
with tools and services,

00:19:26.990 --> 00:19:30.570
the local environment becomes
part of the trust model.

00:19:30.570 --> 00:19:33.210
With local capabilities
discovery for agents,

00:19:33.210 --> 00:19:36.990
we can then complement the
security provided by containers.

00:19:36.990 --> 00:19:40.170
One of the most powerful
capabilities this adds is better

00:19:40.170 --> 00:19:44.610
detection and response; hooks
before tool use, after tool use,

00:19:44.610 --> 00:19:48.330
and at prompt submission; create
stable integration points

00:19:48.330 --> 00:19:51.750
for security products
like Defender to inspect,

00:19:51.750 --> 00:19:54.330
evaluate, and respond.

00:19:54.330 --> 00:19:58.080
That consistent inspection
is essential for customers

00:19:58.080 --> 00:20:00.990
in enterprise environments
to mitigate risks

00:20:00.990 --> 00:20:03.780
like indirect prompt injection.

00:20:03.780 --> 00:20:08.190
Here, a developer asks their
AI agent, GitHub Copilot,

00:20:08.190 --> 00:20:10.950
to investigate and fix an
externally reported issue

00:20:10.950 --> 00:20:12.540
in their repo.

00:20:12.540 --> 00:20:14.850
At first glance, the
issue looks routine.

00:20:14.850 --> 00:20:15.990
But hidden within one

00:20:15.990 --> 00:20:21.120
of its attachments is a
prompt injection attack.

00:20:21.120 --> 00:20:25.800
GitHub Copilot gets to
work on the user's behalf.

00:20:25.800 --> 00:20:30.570
It pulls in the issue and its
attachments from the repository

00:20:30.570 --> 00:20:33.610
and begins analyzing
the content.

00:20:35.030 --> 00:20:42.470
As the agent gets to work, but
before the attack can take hold,

00:20:42.470 --> 00:20:45.450
Microsoft Defender can step in.

00:20:45.450 --> 00:20:48.540
It inspects the data flowing
into the agent in real time,

00:20:48.540 --> 00:20:51.600
recognizes the prompt
injection, and blocks it.

00:20:51.600 --> 00:20:54.510
The detection details, including
the analyzed network prompt

00:20:54.510 --> 00:20:58.540
stream, are surfaced directly
to the user in Windows.

00:21:01.500 --> 00:21:04.080
This protection isn't
limited to GitHub Copilot.

00:21:04.080 --> 00:21:06.210
Agents built with
frameworks like OpenClaw

00:21:06.210 --> 00:21:08.820
that expose tool-calling
hooks are similarly covered.

00:21:08.820 --> 00:21:10.740
The same attack is
attempted; and, once again,

00:21:10.740 --> 00:21:14.010
Defender detects and blocks it.

00:21:14.010 --> 00:21:16.170
For enterprises,
these detections flow

00:21:16.170 --> 00:21:17.880
into the Microsoft
Defender portal,

00:21:17.880 --> 00:21:20.310
providing full visibility
into the attack

00:21:20.310 --> 00:21:24.290
and enabling critical
SecOps workflows.

00:21:24.290 --> 00:21:29.190
Another core principle is
that the agent is not you.

00:21:29.190 --> 00:21:31.410
If agents are going
to do meaningful work

00:21:31.410 --> 00:21:34.860
in enterprise environments,
we need a clear distinction

00:21:34.860 --> 00:21:38.250
between what the human did
and what the agent did.

00:21:38.250 --> 00:21:42.240
Without that distinction,
audit becomes ambiguous,

00:21:42.240 --> 00:21:44.790
permissions become
harder to reason about,

00:21:44.790 --> 00:21:47.940
and accountability
starts to blur.

00:21:47.940 --> 00:21:51.150
Identity matters because
it shapes permissions

00:21:51.150 --> 00:21:55.980
and policy before the action
happens, not just after.

00:21:55.980 --> 00:21:57.540
There are at least
two useful methods

00:21:57.540 --> 00:22:00.300
to identify agents
and their actions.

00:22:00.300 --> 00:22:03.510
Sometimes the right solution
is where the agent acts

00:22:03.510 --> 00:22:05.220
on behalf of the user.

00:22:05.220 --> 00:22:08.760
The agent acts with the
user's delegated context,

00:22:08.760 --> 00:22:11.370
but the action is
still clearly marked

00:22:11.370 --> 00:22:14.490
and attributable
as agent-driven.

00:22:14.490 --> 00:22:16.050
Increasingly, though,

00:22:16.050 --> 00:22:21.150
the stronger model is a unique
agent identity, managed,

00:22:21.150 --> 00:22:23.940
permissioned, and
governed independently

00:22:23.940 --> 00:22:26.820
with more precise
least-privileged controls.

00:22:26.820 --> 00:22:28.080
How should we pick
the best method

00:22:28.080 --> 00:22:31.350
to identify agents
and their actions?

00:22:31.350 --> 00:22:33.630
The delegated path is best

00:22:33.630 --> 00:22:36.750
if you're using
process-based isolation.

00:22:36.750 --> 00:22:38.190
Work that the agent does

00:22:38.190 --> 00:22:41.880
on a human user's behalf
still gets a low-cost agent

00:22:41.880 --> 00:22:44.100
identification tag.

00:22:44.100 --> 00:22:46.740
This allows downstream
systems like Entra

00:22:46.740 --> 00:22:49.530
to apply agentic risk controls.

00:22:49.530 --> 00:22:52.770
The contained Coding Agent
process is a good example

00:22:52.770 --> 00:22:55.140
of using this model.

00:22:55.140 --> 00:22:57.870
If you can go beyond
process-level isolation

00:22:57.870 --> 00:23:00.750
to session-based
isolation, you can benefit

00:23:00.750 --> 00:23:03.600
from using distinct
agent identities.

00:23:03.600 --> 00:23:05.970
Doing so will provide
deep integration

00:23:05.970 --> 00:23:08.520
with the Entra Agent ID system

00:23:08.520 --> 00:23:11.520
that will provide
comprehensive authentication

00:23:11.520 --> 00:23:15.930
and authorization support
for agentic actions.

00:23:15.930 --> 00:23:17.460
Complex interactive agents

00:23:17.460 --> 00:23:21.690
like Claws are an ideal
example of this use case.

00:23:21.690 --> 00:23:26.400
Let's see how the primitives
we talked about come together.

00:23:26.400 --> 00:23:29.730
Earlier, we talked
about the MXC SDK

00:23:29.730 --> 00:23:32.070
and how it can make
containment of different types

00:23:32.070 --> 00:23:35.010
of apps and agents easier.

00:23:35.010 --> 00:23:37.140
In this example, we
have a modified version

00:23:37.140 --> 00:23:40.080
of the open-source
OpenClaw agent.

00:23:40.080 --> 00:23:42.270
Since OpenClaw is a
sophisticated agent,

00:23:42.270 --> 00:23:45.390
session isolation is a much
better fit for the collection

00:23:45.390 --> 00:23:49.740
of components it needs
compared to process isolation.

00:23:49.740 --> 00:23:53.220
I've set up and started our
MXC-integrated OpenClaw.

00:23:53.220 --> 00:23:55.260
The gateway was running earlier,

00:23:55.260 --> 00:23:58.170
and you just saw the
OpenClaw dashboard.

00:23:58.170 --> 00:24:02.520
This should be familiar to
anyone that has tried OpenClaw.

00:24:02.520 --> 00:24:05.640
Let's take one small
look under the covers.

00:24:05.640 --> 00:24:08.550
In the Windows Task
Manager's Users pane,

00:24:08.550 --> 00:24:13.230
you can see we have two users
active on the system: me,

00:24:13.230 --> 00:24:16.050
of course; and my
agent user account.

00:24:16.050 --> 00:24:20.190
OpenClaw's agent loop runs
under the agent user account,

00:24:20.190 --> 00:24:25.350
and you can see its processes
in the Task Manager.

00:24:25.350 --> 00:24:27.000
Apps I use, like the browser

00:24:27.000 --> 00:24:31.800
that hosts the OpenClaw
dashboard, run under my account.

00:24:31.800 --> 00:24:35.770
Let's use the dashboard
with our OpenClaw agent.

00:24:37.140 --> 00:24:38.910
First, I will ask OpenClaw

00:24:38.910 --> 00:24:42.840
to run the Windows
identity utility whoami.

00:24:42.840 --> 00:24:46.620
I can see that this OpenClaw
agent has its own user account

00:24:46.620 --> 00:24:50.260
in the Entra directory
for the tenant.

00:24:51.870 --> 00:24:52.620
This is thanks

00:24:52.620 --> 00:24:56.250
to new provisioning capabilities
introduced in Entra and Intune.

00:24:56.250 --> 00:24:58.470
When OpenClaw was
set up and run,

00:24:58.470 --> 00:25:00.690
the agent session
seamlessly logged

00:25:00.690 --> 00:25:04.290
in with the right Entra agent
user account thanks to updates

00:25:04.290 --> 00:25:07.140
to the Windows identity stack.

00:25:07.140 --> 00:25:09.000
If you recall, the
different session

00:25:09.000 --> 00:25:12.210
and user account gives
us robust isolation.

00:25:12.210 --> 00:25:17.010
Here's a quick look at
my Documents folder.

00:25:17.010 --> 00:25:20.520
If I ask OpenClaw to
list what is there,

00:25:20.520 --> 00:25:25.320
it won't actually see
my Documents folder.

00:25:25.320 --> 00:25:29.580
It will see what it has
in its Documents folder,

00:25:29.580 --> 00:25:34.750
and it should return a result
for a configuration script.

00:25:40.590 --> 00:25:43.590
And, if you look, my Documents
folder has financial

00:25:43.590 --> 00:25:48.360
projections; and the agent
can't automatically access it.

00:25:48.360 --> 00:25:51.480
Now let's have our isolated
agent do something useful

00:25:51.480 --> 00:25:52.560
for us.

00:25:52.560 --> 00:25:54.780
I haven't looked at
that data in a while.

00:25:54.780 --> 00:25:59.730
Let's ask our agent to remind
me what the key details are.

00:25:59.730 --> 00:26:00.750
How do I give it access?

00:26:00.750 --> 00:26:04.320
Well, no special instructions.

00:26:04.320 --> 00:26:09.670
I'll just attach the file like
in any chat with OpenClaw.

00:26:18.180 --> 00:26:21.960
And look. Since this is a
user-authorized action,

00:26:21.960 --> 00:26:24.930
access to the doc will
flow through the agent.

00:26:24.930 --> 00:26:27.360
And it will use various tools

00:26:27.360 --> 00:26:30.120
to give me the insights
I'm looking for,

00:26:30.120 --> 00:26:33.660
all with the right containment
and agent identity constructs,

00:26:33.660 --> 00:26:34.980
working together in Windows

00:26:34.980 --> 00:26:37.800
to provide safer
agentic productivity.

00:26:37.800 --> 00:26:40.290
Additionally, any threats
like prompt injection

00:26:40.290 --> 00:26:44.310
that Defender detects while
using OpenClaw can be associated

00:26:44.310 --> 00:26:48.060
with the Entra agent identity
to facilitate SecOps actions

00:26:48.060 --> 00:26:50.520
in the Defender portal.

00:26:50.520 --> 00:26:53.010
Let me hand back to
Klorida for the close.

00:26:53.010 --> 00:26:54.630
KLORIDA MIRAJ: As we
bring this together,

00:26:54.630 --> 00:26:56.040
the broader point is

00:26:56.040 --> 00:26:59.730
that Windows is not just adding
isolated security features

00:26:59.730 --> 00:27:00.840
for agents.

00:27:00.840 --> 00:27:05.970
It is becoming part of a
much more complete platform.

00:27:05.970 --> 00:27:13.230
Across Windows, Entra, Intune,
Defender, Purview, Agent 365,

00:27:13.230 --> 00:27:18.600
GitHub, and the rest of the
Microsoft system, the story is

00:27:18.600 --> 00:27:23.100
that agent governance
should not be fragmented.

00:27:23.100 --> 00:27:27.420
Every agent on every
Windows device needs a path

00:27:27.420 --> 00:27:31.740
to being discovered,
governed, and audited.

00:27:31.740 --> 00:27:35.280
Identity and containment
are foundational,

00:27:35.280 --> 00:27:37.890
but they are only
the start, right?

00:27:37.890 --> 00:27:40.530
Enterprises also
need centralized

00:27:40.530 --> 00:27:42.480
lifecycle management.

00:27:42.480 --> 00:27:45.990
They need policy
enforcement across devices,

00:27:45.990 --> 00:27:50.850
real-time threat detection and
response, and end-to-end audit

00:27:50.850 --> 00:27:53.460
and compliance tracking.

00:27:53.460 --> 00:27:57.360
It is not enough to know
that an agent exists.

00:27:57.360 --> 00:28:00.540
Organizations need to
manage its lifecycle,

00:28:00.540 --> 00:28:04.350
understand what it can
access, see what it has done,

00:28:04.350 --> 00:28:10.140
and apply policy consistently
across devices and environments.

00:28:10.140 --> 00:28:14.250
Microsoft solutions turn
governance from a concept

00:28:14.250 --> 00:28:16.560
into an operational capability.

00:28:16.560 --> 00:28:21.990
Now, going back to everything
that we discussed today,

00:28:21.990 --> 00:28:27.150
we have a CTA, call to action,
for building agents for you all.

00:28:27.150 --> 00:28:29.340
If you are building agents,

00:28:29.340 --> 00:28:32.940
the practical takeaway
is very simple:

00:28:32.940 --> 00:28:36.600
Design for governance
from the beginning.

00:28:36.600 --> 00:28:42.240
Think about packaging, identity,
containment, network mediation,

00:28:42.240 --> 00:28:47.310
and event emission as core parts
of the system, not as something

00:28:47.310 --> 00:28:49.410
that you've got to do later.

00:28:49.410 --> 00:28:53.970
So a good agent is not just
one that just works, right?

00:28:53.970 --> 00:28:58.410
It is one that can be
identified, monitored,

00:28:58.410 --> 00:29:01.590
and governed in a real
enterprise environment.

00:29:01.590 --> 00:29:04.020
And this is what
excites us most.

00:29:04.020 --> 00:29:06.780
Windows is the best
place to build agents

